# Vauxhall Light Six four-door saloon, 1933-1938.
#
# The Light Six: separate chassis, a 1,530 or 1,781 cc six, a four-speed
# gearbox with the "silent third", and the six-light saloon body. The
# four-door six-light saloon is modelled, being the body the side photograph
# shows; the De Luxe saloon, the coupe, the Tickford and the tourers are not
# represented.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vauxhall_Light_Six
#
# Dimensions: wheelbase 101 in (2.565 m), length 154 in (3.912 m). Width and
# height were not established from a source and are estimated from the
# photograph; the estimate is recorded, not hidden.
#
# Shape reference: image 1 is a true level profile within about 3 degrees,
# taken at waist height with no vertical convergence.
#
# Colours: grade (d); no Vauxhall colour list was found. Black, Maroon, Cream
# and Dark Blue are colours observed on surviving cars, not factory names.
# Weights 12, 10, 9, 8, total 39, provisional.
#
# Speed 110 km/h. Comfort 30/225, on the line 180 + 1.5 x 30. Classes 0,1.
# Dates: intro October 1933; retires staggered 1953-1956 as a road tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/vauxhall-light-six.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/vauxhall-light-six.dae. References:
# citycars/references/vauxhall-light-six/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the late DX profile is modelled, with the black wings
# and the 1936 companion car's covered rear spare, which image 1 does not
# show. The earlier ASX mesh grille and wire wheels are not copied.


